<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Truce of Altmark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1629">1629</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-swedish-war-1626-1629">Polish–Swedish War (1626–1629)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Truce of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a> was a six-year armistice signed on 26 September 1629 at Altmark (Stary Targ) near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-swedish-war-1626-1629">Polish–Swedish War</a> of 1626–1629. Mediated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, it left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and the right to levy tolls in the Prussian ports, while Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a>. The terms freed Gustav II Adolf to intervene in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/truce-of-altmark.jpg" alt="Truce of Altmark" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pawmak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rozejm_polsko-szwedzki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
